What this case does
--------------------
This is step 2 of ../README.md's pipeline. The 2D mesh built in step 1
approximates the airfoil's curved surface with a series of tiny straight
edges (imagine drawing a circle using only short straight line segments --
close, but not actually round). This case fixes exactly that, on the wall
only:

  1. Take the wall's corner points (the coarse points Construct2D itself
     produced) and fit a smooth curve through them -- a cubic spline,
     the same kind of curve you'd get sketching a smooth line through a
     handful of points by eye, but computed exactly.
  2. Find out where that smooth curve actually passes at every point the
     mesh needs (not just the coarse corners -- also the extra points
     Nek5000 adds along each element's edge for higher accuracy).
  3. Write all of that out to a file, ready for step 4 (in
     ../../high_order_mesh/example_nekrs/) to apply to the real 3D mesh.

Nothing here modifies the 2D mesh itself, and nothing here is a real flow
simulation -- this program computes the correction data and exits.

The algorithm (smooth_geom0, in naca_gen_spline_info.usr) is by Paul
Fischer (UIUC / Argonne National Laboratory) -- see the top-level
README's Credits section for the full attribution. It reads meshes this
repo's own pipeline produces (p3d_to_gmsh_nek.py + gmsh2nek + genmap --
see ../README.md), and supports both O-grid and C-grid: on a C-grid, the
wall is only part of one ring of the mesh, not the whole ring -- see
"O-grid vs. C-grid, in this code" in ../README.md, and the
WALL_START_ELEMENT / WALL_ELEMENT_COUNT settings below. This directory is
currently set up for a C-grid case (naca0012_sharp.p3d/.nmf); an O-grid
mesh works too, with different settings (see below).

Running it
----------
 1. Nek5000 requires a specific compile-time setting for this case: open
    SIZE and confirm lx1=4, lxd=5 (already set correctly if you haven't
    touched this file).

 2. Fill in this case's settings (NUMBER_ELEMENTS_X/_Y, WALL_START_ELEMENT,
    WALL_ELEMENT_COUNT, at the top of naca_gen_spline_info.usr) to match
    the actual mesh you're using. Don't work these out by hand -- from
    linear_mesh/, run:

        python3 compute_case_params.py <airfoil>.nmf --smoothing-levels 1 \
            --patch-step1-usr smooth_2D/naca_gen_spline_info.usr

    (NUMBER_ELEMENTS_Y here means "how many rings next to the wall should
    get the smoothing correction", not the mesh's total ring count -- a
    modeling choice, not something derivable from the mesh, which is why
    --smoothing-levels must be given explicitly. 1 -- just the ring
    touching the wall -- is the normal choice.)

 3. Build the 2D mesh this case reads. That's step 1 of ../README.md
    (p3d_to_gmsh_nek.py + Gmsh order elevation + gmsh2nek) -- follow
    "Running it end to end" there, then copy the result in:

        cp <wherever>/airfoil_o2.re2 naca_gen_spline_info.re2

 4. Partition the mesh for Nek5000 (even for a single-process run, this
    step is still required -- it also does some mesh sanity-checking):

        genmap
        # interactive prompts: naca_gen_spline_info, tolerance (e.g. 0.01)

 5. Build and run:

        ./makenek naca_gen_spline_info
        mpirun -np 1 ./nek5000

    This writes geom_3x3_naca_gen_spline_info.dat -- the wall-correction
    data step 4 (in ../../high_order_mesh/example_nekrs/) needs.

    You'll see "EXIT: smooth_geom SUCCESS!" followed by a nonzero shell
    exit code ("an error occured: dying..."). That's expected -- it's
    just how this program signals that it finished successfully, not a
    crash. Check for the success message and the output file above, not
    the exit code.

What's next
-----------
Step 4 (a separate NekRS case, not run from this directory) applies the
correction generated above to the real 3D mesh and runs the actual
simulation. See ../../high_order_mesh/README.md.
